  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 346 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nAs the upper level low slowly tracks east on Thursday, may see some \nlingering showers through the morning into the early afternoon. Can \nnot completely rule out a rumble of thunder or two, but for now, any \nsevere weather risk appears very low. However, with the system \ntracking east, expecting wind conditions across most of the region \non Thursday, with northerly winds of 20 to 30 mph, potentially \ngusting 40 to 45 mph. As the low tracks east, expecting mostly \ncloudy skies throughout the day, again limiting our ability to \nachieve stronger daytime heating. Expecting highs will mostly remain \nin the 50s across the region on Thursday. For reference, our typical \nhighs for mid October are around 65 to 66 degrees. The bigger \ntemperature concerns arrive Thursday night, as mush cooler air \ntracks across portions of western Nebraska. The latest forecast \nbrings lows around freezing across most of Sheridan County, as well \nas portions of western Cherry, northern Garden, and northwest Grant \ncounties. Though there is a chance for localized frost Wednesday \nnight, this push of colder air may bring a more widespread chance \nfor a frost across northwest Nebraska into the western Sandhills. \nWith winds remaining through the night, also expecting a cold start \non Friday morning, with wind chills in the 20s across most of the \nregion. Interestingly enough, this cold push is actually quite \ntypical for mid October, as our typical lows are around 33 to 35 \ndegrees. \n\nSimilar conditions are expected through the day on Friday, with \nhighs mostly remaining in the 50s across the region. However, by \nFriday night, a high pressure system tracks south across the \nnorthern Plains, bringing even cooler temperatures across most of \nthe region, with widespread lows in the low to mid 30s. With lows \ndropping near freezing across most of the region, this increases our \nthreat for a more widespread frost, potentially bringing the first \nfrost of the season across most of western and north central \nNebraska. As this high pressure system tracks south, expecting some \nincreased winds along a tight pressure gradient, which will bring an \neven nastier bite to the cold temperatures. If these conditions come \nto fruition, we may see wind chill values in the low 20s across most \nof the region, especially areas north of Interstate 80, early \nSaturday morning. By Saturday afternoon, however, upper level \nridging is expected to begin building across the region again. This \nshould help return mostly seasonal temperatures on Saturday, with \nhighs climbing into the 60s. In fact, by Sunday, we may be seeing \nhighs returning to the low to mid 70s again, with lows in the\n40s.",
